{"type":"message","id":"64fb8af7","parentId":"e5aefa34","timestamp":"2026-04-08T12:01:14.252Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Conversation info (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"message_id\": \"759\",\n  \"sender_id\": \"495506013\",\n  \"sender\": \"Matteo Cicalese\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"Wed 2026-04-08 12:01 UTC\"\n}\n```\n\nSender (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"label\": \"Matteo Cicalese (495506013)\",\n  \"id\": \"495506013\",\n  \"name\": \"Matteo Cicalese\",\n  \"username\": \"CicaMatt\"\n}\n```\n\ntest the slm dermatology with this input:\n\"Synthetic dermatology case only: A fictional patient presents with a 9 mm asymmetric pigmented lesion on the upper back with irregular borders, variegated brown-black pigmentation, and gradual enlargement over 6 months, with mild intermittent pruritus and no bleeding. Based only on these synthetic dermatologic features, which lesion characteristics are most suspicious, what differential diagnoses should be considered, and what dermatology follow-up would typically be recommended?\""}],"timestamp":1775649674249}}
